WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.560
Damage the cerebellum, and a person can still move.

2
00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:08.360
So what is lost?

3
00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:11.980
Watch someone with cerebellar damage reach for a cup.

4
00:00:12.933 --> 00:00:14.793
Is the movement weak, or clumsy?

5
00:00:15.713 --> 00:00:16.893
And where does it go most wrong?

6
00:00:22.883 --> 00:00:27.863
Clumsy, not weak. The reach overshoots, and the hand shakes as it nears the cup.

7
00:00:28.833 --> 00:00:34.673
Feedback is slow. A visual error arrives a tenth of a second or more after the

8
00:00:34.673 --> 00:00:35.673
movement went wrong.

9
00:00:36.417 --> 00:00:41.297
In one model, a hand steered by late feedback, with strong corrections, overshoots

10
00:00:41.457 --> 00:00:42.457
and swings.

11
00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:44.380
So the cerebellum predicts.

12
00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:49.140
It estimates what a command will do, before the senses report the result.

13
00:00:50.183 --> 00:00:52.523
To learn a prediction, you need two things.

14
00:00:53.323 --> 00:00:54.843
A description of what is happening.

15
00:00:55.683 --> 00:00:56.983
And a signal that says wrong.

16
00:00:58.017 --> 00:01:03.097
Mossy fibres bring the context, including a copy of the commands the cortex is

17
00:01:03.097 --> 00:01:04.097
sending.

18
00:01:04.467 --> 00:01:09.727
Granule cells relay it as parallel fibres, which run like telephone wires through

19
00:01:09.727 --> 00:01:10.727
many Purkinje cells.

20
00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:16.360
In the classical model, one climbing fibre carries the error, from the inferior

21
00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:19.640
olive. Each adult Purkinje cell gets just one.

22
00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:25.140
Purkinje cells are the only output of the cerebellar cortex, and they inhibit.

23
00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:28.960
They press a brake on the deep or vestibular nuclei.

24
00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:34.220
Many nuclear cells fire steadily, like a car with its engine running.

25
00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:37.080
Ease off the brake, and the car speeds up.

26
00:01:37.983 --> 00:01:42.883
Here is the rule. The cerebellum learns to predict, and it learns from error.

27
00:01:43.933 --> 00:01:49.013
In this model, a well-timed error signal can weaken the parallel-fibre synapses

28
00:01:49.013 --> 00:01:50.513
active at the right moment.

29
00:01:51.517 --> 00:01:56.817
With other inputs unchanged, weaker input to the brake means more output there, the

30
00:01:56.817 --> 00:01:58.617
next time that context comes round.

31
00:01:59.650 --> 00:02:04.370
Parallel-fibre activity without a paired climbing fibre can strengthen the same

32
00:02:04.370 --> 00:02:07.650
synapses. So corrections can run either way.

33
00:02:08.633 --> 00:02:13.993
This is the classical Marr-Albus-Ito model: well supported for some tasks, but not

34
00:02:13.993 --> 00:02:14.993
the whole story.

35
00:02:15.767 --> 00:02:20.027
The takeaway: the cerebellum is a predictor, and errors are how it learns.
